{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]],"date-time":"2026-09-30T08:22:21Z","timestamp":1790756541126,"version":"4.1.0"},"reference-count":72,"publisher":"IOP Publishing","issue":"5","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2017,4,7]],"date-time":"2017-04-07T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1491523200000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2017,4,7]],"date-time":"2017-04-07T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1491523200000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https://iopscience.iop.org/info/page/text-and-data-mining"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["iopscience.iop.org"],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["J. Phys. G: Nucl. Part. Phys."],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,5,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    The conventional hadron-resonance gas (HRG) model with the Particle Data Group hadron input, full chemical equilibrium, and the hadron-type dependent eigenvolume interactions, is employed to fit the hadron midrapidity yield data of the ALICE collaboration for the most central Pb+Pb collisions. For the case of point-like hadrons, the well-known fit result\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mi>T</mml:mi>\n                        <mml:mo>=</mml:mo>\n                        <mml:mn>154</mml:mn>\n                        <mml:mo>±</mml:mo>\n                        <mml:mn>2</mml:mn>\n                        <mml:mspace width=\"0.25em\"/>\n                        <mml:mi>MeV</mml:mi>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    is reproduced. However, the situation changes if the hadrons have different eigenvolumes. In the case when all the mesons are point-like while all the baryons have an effective hardcore radius of 0.3 fm, the temperature dependence of\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:msup>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>χ</mml:mi>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mn>2</mml:mn>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                        </mml:msup>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    has a broad minimum in the temperature range of 155−210 MeV, with a fit quality comparable to the\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:mi>T</mml:mi>\n                        <mml:mo>∼</mml:mo>\n                        <mml:mn>155</mml:mn>\n                        <mml:mspace width=\"0.25em\"/>\n                        <mml:mi>MeV</mml:mi>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    minimum in the point-particle case. A very similar result is obtained when only baryon–baryon eigenvolume interactions are considered, with the eigenvolume parameter taken from the previous fit to the ground state of nuclear matter. Finally, when we apply the eigenvolume corrections with a mass-proportional eigenvolume\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:msub>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>v</mml:mi>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>i</mml:mi>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                        </mml:msub>\n                        <mml:mo>∼</mml:mo>\n                        <mml:msub>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>m</mml:mi>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>i</mml:mi>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                        </mml:msub>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    , fixed to a particular hardcore proton radius\n                    <jats:italic>r</jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sub>\n                      <jats:italic>p</jats:italic>\n                    </jats:sub>\n                    , we observe a second minimum in the temperature dependence of \n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:tex-math>\n                        \n                      </jats:tex-math>\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" overflow=\"scroll\">\n                        <mml:msup>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mi>χ</mml:mi>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                          <mml:mrow>\n                            <mml:mn>2</mml:mn>\n                          </mml:mrow>\n                        </mml:msup>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </jats:inline-formula>\n                    , located at significantly higher temperatures. For instance, at\n                    <jats:italic>r</jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sub>\n                      <jats:italic>p</jats:italic>\n                    </jats:sub>\n                    = 0.5 fm, the fit quality is better than in the point-particle HRG case in a very wide temperature range of 170−320 MeV, which creates uncertainty in the temperature determination from the fit to the data of 150 MeV. These results show that thermal fits to the heavy-ion hadron yield data are very sensitive to the modeling of the short-range repulsion eigenvolume between hadrons, and that the chemical freeze-out temperature can only be extracted from the LHC hadron yield data with sizable uncertainty.\n                  </jats:p>","DOI":"10.1088/1361-6471/aa6409","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2017,3,2]],"date-time":"2017-03-02T07:15:15Z","timestamp":1488438915000},"page":"055103","update-policy":"https://doi.org/10.1088/crossmark-policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":31,"title":["Surprisingly large uncertainties in temperature extraction from thermal fits to hadron yield data at LHC"],"prefix":"10.1088","volume":"44","author":[{"ORCID":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2189-4766","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Volodymyr","family":"Vovchenko","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Frankfurt Institute for Advanced Studies, Goethe Universität Frankfurt"},{"name":"Taras Shevchenko National University of Kiev"},{"name":"Institut für Theoretische Physik, Goethe Universität Frankfurt"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Horst","family":"Stoecker","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Frankfurt Institute for Advanced Studies, Goethe Universität Frankfurt"},{"name":"Institut für Theoretische Physik, Goethe Universität Frankfurt"},{"name":"GSI Helmholtzzentrum für Schwerionenforschung GmbH"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"266","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2017,4,7]]},"reference":[{"key":"